  "worked_solution": "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-2\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{15}{128}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{15}{128}(8x-4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{32}(2x-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{32}x(2-{x}^{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{32}x(2-{x}^{2})=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-2,2],$ so we consider them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm \\sqrt{2},\\pm 2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =0 \\\\ f(-\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{4})=\\frac{15}{128}(8-4)=\\frac{15}{32} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(0)}^{2}-{(0)}^{4})=0 \\\\ f(\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{2})}^{4})=\\frac{15}{128}(8-4)=\\frac{15}{32} \\\\ f(2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-\\sqrt{2})=f(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{15}{32}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$",
